  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Two rods, one of aluminium and the other made of steel, having initial lengths&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> are connected together to form a single rod of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>. The coefficients of linear expansion for aluminium and steel are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">α</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">α</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;respectively. If the length of each rod increases by the same amount when their temperature is raised by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>, then find the ratio&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></math></p>\r"
  },
